2. Blood viscosity increases the pressure required to maintain blood flow. This is because the blood has a higher resistance to flow, so more pressure is needed to overcome this resistance and maintain the same flow rate.

In summary, an increase in blood viscosity can lead to slower blood flow and higher blood pressure. This can lead to various health issues, such as constipation, dehydration, and other symptoms.
