</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
The key clinical features of poliovirus infection include initial oropharyngeal infection, followed by spread to the spinal cord anterior horn cells and bulbar nuclei. This can lead to the hallmark complication of **paralysis**, which is typical of polio. The virus is transmitted via the **fecal-oral route**, and in regions with routine vaccination, the disease is rare. Other complications may include muscle weakness and, in severe cases, respiratory failure due to involvement of the respiratory muscles. 

