","To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction, we can use the formula:

ΔH = Σ ΔHf(products) - Σ ΔHf(reactants)

where ΔHf is the standard enthalpy of formation for each substance.

For the given reaction:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The products are Ca(OH)2 (s), and the reactants are CaO (s) and H2O (l).

The standard enthalpies of formation are:

ΔHf(CaO(s)) = -635.1 kJ/mol
ΔHf(H2O(l)) = -285.8 kJ/mol
ΔHf(Ca(OH)2(s)) = -986.2 kJ/mol

Now, we can calculate the standard enthalpy change:

ΔH = ΔHf(Ca(OH)2(s)) - [ΔHf(CaO(s)) + ΔHf(H2O(l))]

