   \[
   \text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 10 \text{ kmph} \times 3 \text{ h} = 30 \text{ km}
   \]
   After 3 hours, a is 30 km away from the start.

2. **Determine the relative speed of b relative to a**:
   Since b is going at 20 kmph and a is going at 10 kmph, the relative speed of b relative to a is:
   \[
   \text{Relative speed} = 20 \text{ kmph} - 10 \text{ kmph} = 10 \text{ kmph}
   \]

3. **Calculate the time it takes for b to catch up to a**:
   The distance b must cover to catch up to a is 30 km (the distance a is away from the start). Using the relative speed:
   \[
   \text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Relative speed}} = \frac{30 \text{ km}}{10 \text{ kmph}} = 3 \text{ h}
   \]

4. **Calculate the distance from the start when b catches up to a**:
   Since b starts 3 hours after a, the total time b is traveling is 3 hours. The distance b travels in 3 hours is:
   \[
   \text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 20 \text{ kmph} \times 3 \text{ h} = 60 \text{ km}
   \]

