","To prove that the Riemann-Liouville fractional derivative operators \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) do not commute in general, we must show that \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) \neq aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \) for any functions \( f(t) \) and constants \( a, p, q \). 

1. **Non-Commomination**:
   The Riemann-Liouville fractional derivative is defined as:
   \[
   aD_t^p f(t) = \frac{a}{t^p} \int_0^t (t - t_0)^p f(t_0) \, dt_0, \quad 0 < p < 1.
   \]
   For \( p = 1 \), it is the standard derivative. 

2. **Non-Commutativity**:
   The non-commutativity of \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) is proven by considering the difference \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) - aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \). 

3. **Final Answer**:
   The conditions under which \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) commute are:
   \[
   \int_0^t (t - t_0)^p f(t_0) \, dt_0 = 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \int_0^t (t - t_0)^q f(t_0) \, dt_0 = 0.
   \]
   This ensures that the lower terminal conditions vanish, ensuring the equality of the two expressions. 

Thus, the conditions for the Ricomb-Liouville fractional derivatives to commute are:
\[
\int_0^t (t - t_0)^p f(t_0) \, dt_0 = 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \int_0^t (t - t_0)^q f(t_0) \, dt_0 = 0.
\]
This ensures the equality of the two expressions, ensuring the commutativity of the two operators. 

\boxed{
\text{The Ricoli-Liouville fractional derivatives } aD_t^p \text{ and } aD_t^q \text{ do not commute in general.} \\
\text{They commute if } \int_0^t (t - t_0)^p f(t_0) \, dt_0 = 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \int_0^t (t_0 - t)^q f(t_0) \, dt_0 = 0.
