","To find the maximum possible change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron, we can use the Compton effect formula:

\[
\lambda' = \lambda (1 - \frac{m_e c^2}{E} + \frac{2m_e c^2}{E} \sin^2 \theta)
\]

where:
- \(\lambda\) is the initial wavelength of the photon (2.00 Å)
- \(\lambda'\) is the scattered wavelength of the photon
- \(m_e\) is the mass of the electron (9.11 x 10^{-31} kg)
- \(c\) is the speed of light (3.00 x 10^8 m/s)
- \(E\) is the energy of the incident photon
- \(\theta\) is the scattering angle

The maximum change in wavelength occurs when \(\sin^2 \theta = 1\), which occurs at \(\theta = 90^\circ\). Substituting \(\sin^2 \theta = 1\) into the Compton formula:

\[
\lambda' = \lambda (1 - \frac{m_e c^2}{E} + 2m_e c^2/E) = \lambda (1 + \frac{m_e c^2}{E})
\]

The energy of the incident photon is:

\[
E = \frac{hc}{\lambda} = \frac{(6.63 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s})(3.00 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s})}{2.00 \times 10^{-10} \text{ m}} = 1.00 \times 10^{-19} \text{ J}
\]

The energy of the electron is:

\[
m_e c^2 = (9.11 \times 10^{-31} \text{ kg})(3.00 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s})^2 = 8.16 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}
\]

Substituting these values:

\[
\lambda' = 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \text{ m} \left(1 + \frac{8.16 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}}{1.00 \times 10^{-19} \text{ J}}\right) = 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \text{ m} (1 + 8.16 \times 10^6)
\]

\[
\lambda' = 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \text{ m} (8.16 \times 10^6 + 1) = 1.632 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}
\]

The change in wavelength is:

\[
\Delta \lambda = \lambda' - \lambda = 1.632 \times 11^{-3} \text{ m} - 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \text{ m} \approx 1.632 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}
\]

The percentage change in the wavelength is:

\[
\frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda} \times 100\%",relevance,"# Task Description
