</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The magnitude of the acceleration of the proton is calculated using Newton's second law:  
\[ F = m a \]  
The force on the proton is given by:  
\[ F = q E \]  
where \( q = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C} \) and \( E = 500 \, \text{N/C} \).  
Substituting the values:  
\[ F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 800 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{N} \]  
Now, solve for acceleration:  
\[ a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{800 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.81 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2 \]  
The magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.81 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) The proton starts from rest, so \( v_0 = 0 \). Using the kinematic equation:  
\[ v = v_0 + a t \]  
Rearranging to solve for time:  
\[ t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0}{4.81 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.25 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s} \]  
The time taken is \(\boxed{7.25 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}}\).
