\[
S = U^\dagger U,
\]

where \(U\) is the unitary matrix that relates the initial and final states of a process. The matrix elements of the \(S\)-matrix are given by:

\[
S_{lm} = \langle n | U^\dagger U | n' \rangle = \langle n | U^\dagger | n' \rangle \langle n | U | n' \rangle.
\]

The unitarity condition for the \(S\)-matrix is:

\[
\sum_l S_{lm}^* S_{ln} = \delta_{mn}.
\]

Substituting the definition of \(S_{lm}\) into the unitarity condition:

\[
\sum_l \langle n | U^\dagger | n \rangle^* \langle n | U | n' \rangle^* \langle n | U^\dagger | l \rangle \langle n | U | l \rangle = \delta_{mn}.
\]

Since \(U\) is unitary, \(U^\dagger U = I\), so:

\[
\sum_l \langle n | U^\dagger U | n \rangle^* \langle n | U^\dagger U | n' \rangle^* \langle n | U^\dagger | l \rangle \langle n n' | U | l \rangle = \delta_{mn}.
\]

Using the orthogonality of states, \(\langle n | n \rangle = 1\), and \(\langle n | n' \rangle = \delta_{nm}\):

\[
\sum_l \langle n | n \rangle^* \langle n | n' \rangle^* \langle n | U^\dagger | l \rangle \langle n n' | U | l \rangle = \delta_{mn}.
\]

Simplifying:

\[
\sum_l 1 \cdot \delta_{nm}^* \langle n | U^\dagger | l \rangle \langle n n' | U | l \rangle = \delta_{mn}.
\]

Since \(\delta_{nm}'\) is the same as \(\delta_{nm}\), we simplify further:

\[
\sum_l \delta_{nm} \langle n | U^\dagger | l \rangle \langle n n' | U | l \rangle = \delta_{mn}.
\]

Using the orthogonality of states again, we can rewrite the inner products as:

\[
\sum_l \delta_{nm} \langle n | U^\dagger | l \rangle \langle n' | U | l \rangle = \delta_{mn}.
\]

Using the cyclic property of the trace:

\[
\sum_l \delta_{nm} \langle n | U^\dagger | l \rangle \langle n' | U | l \rangle = \delta_{nm} \sum_l \langle n | U^\dagger | l \rangle \langle n' | U | l \rangle = \delta_{mn}.
\]

This simplifies to:

\[
\delta_{nm} \sum_l \langle n | U^\dagger | l \rangle \langle n' | U | l \rangle = \delta_{nm}.
