An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The closed-loop voltage gain \( A_{vf} \) is given by:
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 + A_v m_v}
\]
Substituting \( A_v = 100 \) and \( m_v = 0.1 \):
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{100}{1 + 100 \times 0.1} = \frac{100}{1 + 10} = \frac{100}{11} \approx 9.09 \text{ V/V}
\]
The gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to aging. The new gain \( A_v' \) is:
\[
A_v' = \frac{A_v}{2} = \frac{100}{2} = 50 \text{ V/V}
\]
The new closed-loop voltage gain \( A_{vf}' \) is:
\[
A_{vf}' = \frac{A_v'}{1 + A_v' m_v} = \frac{50}{1 + 50 \times 0.1} = \frac{50}{1 + 5} = \frac{50}{6} \approx 8.33 \text{ V/V}
\]
The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is:
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{8.33 - 9.09}{9.09} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{-0.76}{9.09} \right) \times 100 \approx -8.3\%
\]
Thus, the percentage change in the closed-loop gain is \(\boxed{-8.3\%}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
