","The scattered particle is assumed to be a particle of mass \( m \), with initial four-momentum \( P_i = (E_i/c, \mathbf{p}_i) \) and final four-momentum \( P_f = (E_f/c, \mathbf{p}_f) \). The electron is the scattered particle in this case. The scattered particle's momentum transfer is given by the difference between the initial and final four-momenta:

\[
Q = P_i - P_f = \left( \frac{E_i}{c}, \mathbf{p}_i \right) - \left( \frac{E_f}{c}, \mathbf{p}_f \right) = \left( \frac{E_i - E_f}{c}, \mathbf{p}_i - \mathbf{p}_f \right).
\]

The square of the four-momentum transfer is:

\[
Q^2 = (P_i - P_f)^2 = \left( \frac{E_i - E_f}{c} \right)^2 - \left( \mathbf{p}_i - \mathbf{p}_f \right)^2.
\]

This is the definition of \( Q^2 \) in the context of elastic scattering. 

Thus, the squared four-momentum transfer is:

\[
\boxed{Q^2 = \left( \frac{E_i - E_f}{c} \right)^2 - \left( \mathbf{p}_i - \mathbf{f} \right)^2}.
