- \(g_n\) is the normal gravity
- \(G\) is the gravitational constant (\(6.6743 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{m}^3 \, \text{kg}^{-1} \, \text{s}^{-2}\))
- \(M\) is the Earth's mass (\(5.9761 \times 10^{24} \, \text{kg}\))
- \(a\) is the Earth's equatorial radius
- \(z\) is the distance from the point P to the Earth's center

**Step 1: Calculate the Earth's equatorial radius \(a\)**

The distance from the center to the point P is given as 6362.121 km. This is the Earth's radius \(a\) in the equatorial plane. Thus, \(a = 6362.121 \, \text{km} = 6,362,121 \, \text{m}\).

